+\begin{ALblock}
+An elementary count makes this precise. Let $\mmat{X}_A \in \reals^{m_A \times n_A}$ and
+$\mmat{X}_B \in \reals^{m_B \times n_B}$, and let $s = |\mvec{r}_A \cap \mvec{r}_B|$ be the
+number of shared rows. The symbolic pass of \eqref{eq:pd-product} merges two sorted
+index vectors in $O(m_A + m_B)$ operations and allocates the $n_B \times n_A$ result. The
+numeric pass performs $n_B\, s\, n_A$ multiply-adds in one \texttt{dgemm}. For a stacked
+operand, both counts are summed over the pairs of blocks with intersecting rows. If the same
+blocks were stored as generic sparse matrices, Gustavson's algorithm would perform the same
+$n_B\, s\, n_A$ multiply-adds, but its symbolic pass would build the full
+pattern of the result, with $O(n_B\, s\, n_A)$ operations and one stored index per
+entry. The dense representations therefore reduce the symbolic pass and the index storage
+from the number of entries of a block to the number of its rows and columns. In the
+numeric pass they keep the same operation count and replace the index-driven sparse loop
+by a BLAS call, a gain in the constant factor rather than in the asymptotic cost. When one
+operand is dense and the other is generic sparse, the numeric pass costs one multiply-add
+per stored nonzero of the sparse operand in the shared rows, per column of the dense
+block.
+\end{ALblock}
